he other diagonal holds the cells with y+x = 16. It runs from (2,14) to (14,2). The added cells match these exactly.\n- **Pair 2, pixel (3,3).** The y−x = 0 diagonal runs from (0,0) to (14,14). The y+x = 6 diagonal runs from (0,6) to (6,0). This matches.\n- **Pair 3, pixel (3,2).** The y−x = 1 diagonal runs from (1,0) to (6,5). The y+x = 5 diagonal runs from (0,5) to (5,0). This matches.\n\nAll ray cells take the pixel's colour, and nothing else in the grid changes.\n\n**Building the output.** I first paint the main diagonal through the pixel, the cells where y−x is the same as at the pixel. Then I paint the anti-diagonal, the cells where y+x is the same as at the pixel. Each diagonal is clipped to the grid. I emit the canvas after each of these two steps. The pixel's position, its colour and the grid size are all read from the input, so the 17×17 test grid needs no special handling.\n\n**The test input.** It has a 6 at (7,12) in a 17×17 grid.\n- The main diagonal y−x = −5 runs from (0,5) to (11,16).\n- The anti-diagonal y+x = 19 runs from (3,16) to (16,3).","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    h, w = g.shape\n    ys, xs = np.nonzero(g != bg)\n    py, px = int(ys[0]), int(xs[0])\n    c = int(g[py, px])\n    out = g.copy()\n    # main diagonal through the pixel (y - x constant)\n    for y in range(h):\n        x = y - py + px\n        if 0 <= x < w:\n            out[y, x] = c\n    emit(out, f\"Draw the down-right diagonal through the pixel at ({py},{px}) in colour {c}, edge to edge.\")\n    # anti-diagonal through the pixel (y + x constant)\n    for y in range(h):\n        x = py + px - y\n        if 0 <= x < w:\n            out[y, x] = c\n    emit(out, \"Draw the down-left diagonal through the same pixel, completing the X to the grid borders.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rays":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Four diagonal rays now leave it, and each ray continues until it reaches the edge of the grid."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"nd up-left, each running from \"next to the input colour\" to \"at the grid border.\" Together the four rays make a large X centred on the pixel."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"All ray cells take the pixel's colour, and nothing else in the grid changes."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"42952db852e1c6c4070e59ac50de0833b8ba2e7b943dd58889f000bf0fb2d38a","task_sha256":"4b47d7f4f79e0ec62deb7feb4af4e7f7554ab37d66f854090a26f9d5c89cbca2","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
